\title{Korselt's Criterion and a Multiplicative-Order Bridge\\
to Cryptographic Pseudoprimality}
\author{Harmonic}
\date{2025-06-15}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Korselt's criterion characterizes the absolute Fermat pseudoprimes (Carmichael
numbers) as the squarefree composites $n$ for which $(p-1)\mid(n-1)$ for every
prime $p\mid n$. We isolate, state, and rigorously establish the precise
arithmetic step at the heart of the criterion's converse direction: if every unit
modulo a squarefree $n$ is annihilated by the exponent $n-1$, then $(p-1)\mid(n-1)$
for every prime $p\mid n$. We show that this step is, structurally, a statement
about orders of elements in finite abelian groups. The proof combines four
ingredients: (i) order divides any uniform annihilating exponent, with no
finiteness hypothesis; (ii) group homomorphisms do not increase orders; (iii) the
reduction homomorphism $\units{n}\twoheadrightarrow\units{p}$ is surjective
whenever $p\mid n$; and (iv) $\units{p}$ is cyclic of order $p-1$ and hence
contains an element of order exactly $p-1$. We discuss how this bridge separates
the global pseudoprimality condition from the local order condition, why the
exponent $n-1$ is inessential (pointing to the Carmichael function $\lambda(n)$ as
the true invariant), and the consequences for primality testing, including the
rationale for the Miller--Rabin refinement. We close with falsifiable conjectures:
the full converse of Korselt's criterion, a generalized-exponent version, and a
quantitative order-collapse statement underpinning randomized compositeness
witnesses.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Korselt's criterion, Carmichael numbers, Fermat
pseudoprimes, multiplicative group of units, element order, cyclic groups,
primality testing, Miller--Rabin, Carmichael function.

