\section{Introduction}

Fermat's Little Theorem states that for a prime $p$ and an integer $a$ with
$p\nmid a$, one has $a^{p-1}\equiv 1\pmod p$. The contrapositive yields the
\emph{Fermat primality test}: if $a^{n-1}\not\equiv 1\pmod n$ for some base $a$
coprime to $n$, then $n$ is composite. The test is fast and factorization-free,
but it admits false positives.

A composite $n>1$ is an \emph{absolute Fermat pseudoprime} (a \emph{Carmichael
number}) if $a^{n-1}\equiv 1\pmod n$ for \emph{every} integer $a$ coprime to $n$.
Such $n$ pass the Fermat test for all admissible bases. The classical
characterization is:

\begin{theorem}[Korselt, 1899]
A composite $n>1$ is a Carmichael number if and only if $n$ is squarefree and
$(p-1)\mid(n-1)$ for every prime $p\mid n$.
\end{theorem}

The criterion is a biconditional with two asymmetric directions. The
\emph{forward} (constructive) direction---squarefree and $\forall p\mid n,\,
(p-1)\mid(n-1)$ implies absolute Fermat pseudoprime---is a Chinese Remainder
Theorem (CRT) computation. The \emph{converse} direction must \emph{produce} the
divisibility from the global hypothesis, and it is here that the multiplicative
group structure does the work. This paper isolates the single arithmetic core of
the converse and gives a self-contained, group-theoretic proof.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
